WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-ES

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:03.159
How the economic machine works, in 30 minutes.

00:00:03.159 --> 00:00:06.680
The economy works like a simple machine.

00:00:06.680 --> 00:00:09.390
However, many do not understand it

00:00:09.390 --> 00:00:11.490
— or disagree on how it works

00:00:11.490 --> 00:00:15.150
— and that has led to much needless economic suffering.

00:00:15.150 --> 00:00:18.249
I feel a deep obligation

00:00:18.249 --> 00:00:22.049
to share my economic template, simple but practical.

00:00:23.049 --> 00:00:25.320
Although unconventional, it

00:00:25.320 --> 00:00:30.150
helped me predict and avoid the global financial crisis,

00:00:30.150 --> 00:00:32.460
and it has served me well for over 30 years.

00:00:32.460 --> 00:00:34.260
Let's begin, then.

00:00:34.260 --> 00:00:39.520
Although economics may seem complex, it works in a simple and mechanical way.

00:00:39.520 --> 00:00:43.930
It consists of a few simple pieces and many simple transactions

00:00:43.930 --> 00:00:47.260
that are repeated over and over again, countless times.

00:00:47.260 --> 00:00:51.460
These transactions are driven, more than anything, by human nature,

00:00:51.460 --> 00:00:54.800
and create 3 main forces that move the economy.

00:00:55.800 --> 00:00:58.120
One: Increased productivity

00:00:58.120 --> 00:01:01.650
Two: The short-term debt cycle

00:01:01.650 --> 00:01:04.400
Three: The long-term debt cycle

00:01:04.400 --> 00:01:07.770
We will look at these three forces and how, by overlapping them,

00:01:07.770 --> 00:01:11.530
we create a good template for tracking economic movements

00:01:11.530 --> 00:01:13.590
and understanding what is happening.

00:01:13.590 --> 00:01:16.830
Let's start with the simplest part of economics:

00:01:16.830 --> 00:01:18.860
transactions.

00:01:19.340 --> 00:01:23.840
An economy is simply the sum of the transactions that comprise it,

00:01:23.840 --> 00:01:26.570
and a transaction is something very simple.  We're

00:01:26.570 --> 00:01:28.360
constantly making transactions.

00:01:28.360 --> 00:01:31.760
Every time we buy something, we create a transaction.

00:01:31.760 --> 00:01:35.240
Each transaction consists of a buyer

00:01:35.240 --> 00:01:37.150
exchanging money or credit

00:01:37.150 --> 00:01:42.550
with a seller for goods, services, or financial assets.

00:01:42.550 --> 00:01:45.150
Credit is spent like money,

00:01:45.150 --> 00:01:49.280
so by adding the money spent to the credit spent

00:01:49.280 --> 00:01:52.160
we know the total expenditure.

00:01:52.160 --> 00:01:55.560
Total spending drives the economy.

00:01:55.560 --> 00:01:57.590
If we divide the sum spent

00:01:57.590 --> 00:01:59.990
by the quantity sold,

00:01:59.990 --> 00:02:01.290
we get the price.

00:02:01.290 --> 00:02:04.380
And that's all.  This is a transaction.

00:02:04.480 --> 00:02:07.110
It is the pillar of the economic machine.

00:02:07.110 --> 00:02:12.019
The cycles and forces of an economy are driven by transactions.

00:02:12.019 --> 00:02:14.469
So if we understand transactions,

00:02:14.469 --> 00:02:16.569
we understand the whole economy.

00:02:16.569 --> 00:02:19.510
A market consists of all buyers

00:02:19.510 --> 00:02:20.510
and all sellers

00:02:20.510 --> 00:02:22.910
who transact with the same thing.

00:02:23.110 --> 00:02:25.540
For example, there is a wheat market,

00:02:25.540 --> 00:02:26.840
a car market,

00:02:26.840 --> 00:02:27.640
a stock market,

00:02:27.640 --> 00:02:29.550
and markets for millions of other things.

00:02:31.050 --> 00:02:33.470
An economy consists of all transactions

00:02:33.470 --> 00:02:34.670
in all its markets.

00:02:34.670 --> 00:02:37.550
If we add up the total expenses

00:02:37.550 --> 00:02:39.450
and the total amount sold

00:02:39.450 --> 00:02:40.430
in all markets,

00:02:40.430 --> 00:02:42.030
we have everything we need to know

00:02:42.030 --> 00:02:43.630
to understand the economy.

00:02:44.530 --> 00:02:45.960
It's that simple.

00:02:46.560 --> 00:02:49.430
Individuals, businesses, banks, and governments

00:02:49.430 --> 00:02:53.730
all participate in transactions as I just described:

00:02:53.730 --> 00:02:58.530
they exchange money and credit for goods, services, and financial assets.

00:02:59.030 --> 00:03:01.500
The largest buyer and seller is the government,

00:03:02.600 --> 00:03:04.040
made up of two important parts:

00:03:04.040 --> 00:03:07.690
a central government that collects taxes and spends money...

00:03:07.690 --> 00:03:09.669
...and a central bank,

00:03:09.669 --> 00:03:12.269
which is different from other buyers and sellers because

00:03:12.269 --> 00:03:15.989
it controls the amount of money and credit in the economy.  It does this by

00:03:16.689 --> 00:03:19.650
influencing interest rates

00:03:19.650 --> 00:03:21.020
and printing new money.

00:03:21.020 --> 00:03:23.620
For these reasons, as we will see,

00:03:24.020 --> 00:03:27.200
the Central Bank is an important player in the flow

00:03:27.700 --> 00:03:28.700
of credit.

00:03:29.400 --> 00:03:31.629
Now, let's focus on credit.

00:03:31.629 --> 00:03:34.659
Credit is the most important part of the economy,

00:03:34.659 --> 00:03:36.539
and probably the least understood.   It

00:03:37.039 --> 00:03:40.319
is the most important part because it is the largest

00:03:40.319 --> 00:03:42.100
and most volatile.

00:03:42.600 --> 00:03:45.100
Just as buyers and sellers go to the market to make transactions, so do

00:03:47.100 --> 00:03:49.969
lenders and borrowers.

00:03:49.969 --> 00:03:53.569
Lenders usually want to turn their money into more money,

00:03:53.569 --> 00:03:57.260
and borrowers usually want to buy something they can't afford,

00:03:57.260 --> 00:03:59.059
like a house or a car,

00:03:59.059 --> 00:04:02.319
or they want to invest in something, like starting a business.

00:04:03.019 --> 00:04:04.599
Credit can help both lenders

00:04:04.599 --> 00:04:07.799
and borrowers get what they want.

00:04:08.099 --> 00:04:11.560
Borrowers promise to repay the borrowed sum,

00:04:11.560 --> 00:04:12.719
called "the principal",

00:04:12.719 --> 00:04:15.419
plus an additional sum called "interest".

00:04:15.419 --> 00:04:17.560
When interest rates are high,

00:04:17.560 --> 00:04:20.560
fewer loans are taken out because they are expensive.

00:04:20.560 --> 00:04:22.540
When interest rates are low,

00:04:22.540 --> 00:04:25.040
more loans are taken out because they are cheaper.

00:04:25.040 --> 00:04:27.930
When borrowers promise to repay the loan

00:04:28.330 --> 00:04:29.959
and lenders believe them,

00:04:29.959 --> 00:04:31.059
credit is created.

00:04:31.659 --> 00:04:35.170
Any two people can agree to create credit out of thin air.

00:04:35.770 --> 00:04:39.210
This seems simple enough, but credit is elusive

00:04:39.210 --> 00:04:40.499
because it has different names.

00:04:40.499 --> 00:04:43.599
Once the credit is generated, it

00:04:43.599 --> 00:04:45.029
immediately becomes debt.

00:04:46.229 --> 00:04:48.670
Debt is an asset for the lender

00:04:48.670 --> 00:04:50.770
and a liability for the borrower.

00:04:51.370 --> 00:04:52.540
In the future,

00:04:52.540 --> 00:04:56.240
when the borrower repays the loan with interest, the

00:04:56.240 --> 00:04:58.549
asset and liability will disappear

00:04:58.549 --> 00:05:00.949
and the transaction will be settled.

00:05:01.749 --> 00:05:04.629
So why is credit so important?

00:05:05.229 --> 00:05:07.619
Because when a borrower obtains credit, it

00:05:07.619 --> 00:05:09.719
can increase their expenses.

00:05:09.719 --> 00:05:13.349
And we must remember: spending drives the economy.

00:05:13.349 --> 00:05:15.699
That's because one person's spending

00:05:15.699 --> 00:05:17.099
is another person's income.

00:05:17.499 --> 00:05:22.189
Let's remember that for every dollar we spend, someone earns it.

00:05:22.189 --> 00:05:25.279
And every dollar we earned was spent by someone.

00:05:25.279 --> 00:05:29.149
So when we spend more, someone earns more.

00:05:29.149 --> 00:05:32.649
When someone's income increases,

00:05:32.649 --> 00:05:34.649
lenders are more willing to lend them money

00:05:34.649 --> 00:05:37.629
because they are now more creditworthy.

00:05:37.629 --> 00:05:40.929
A creditworthy borrower has two things:

00:05:40.929 --> 00:05:43.519
the ability to repay the loan and a guarantor.

00:05:44.319 --> 00:05:48.319
Having a high income, compared to the debt, allows you to pay back money.

00:05:49.079 --> 00:05:55.749
If he is unable to do so, he has valuable assets as collateral that can be sold.

00:05:55.749 --> 00:06:00.340
This makes lenders feel comfortable lending you money.

00:06:00.440 --> 00:06:03.960
Thus, higher incomes allow for taking out more loans,

00:06:03.960 --> 00:06:05.680
which in turn allow for greater spending.

00:06:05.680 --> 00:06:09.920
And since one person's expenses are another person's income,

00:06:09.920 --> 00:06:13.360
this leads to more loans and so on.

00:06:13.360 --> 00:06:17.350
This behavior is self-reinforcing and leads to economic growth, which is why

00:06:17.350 --> 00:06:21.640
we have cycles.

00:06:21.640 --> 00:06:25.620
In a transaction, you have to give something to get something,

00:06:25.620 --> 00:06:28.830
and how much you get depends on how much you produce.

00:06:29.330 --> 00:06:31.430
Over time we learn,

00:06:31.430 --> 00:06:34.680
and that accumulated knowledge raises the standards of living.  We

00:06:34.680 --> 00:06:36.930
call this productivity growth.

00:06:37.430 --> 00:06:40.430
Those who are creative and hardworking increase

00:06:40.430 --> 00:06:43.540
their productivity and living standards more quickly

00:06:43.540 --> 00:06:46.950
than those who are complacent and lazy,

00:06:48.850 --> 00:06:49.710
although not necessarily in the short term.

00:06:49.710 --> 00:06:54.340
Productivity matters more in the long run;  short-term credit.

00:06:54.340 --> 00:06:58.370
This is because productivity growth does not fluctuate much,

00:06:58.370 --> 00:07:01.480
so it is not a major driver of economic swings.

00:07:01.480 --> 00:07:06.750
Debt is indeed a problem, because acquiring it allows us to consume more than we produce

00:07:06.750 --> 00:07:11.050
and forces us to consume less than we produce when we repay it.

00:07:11.050 --> 00:07:14.770
The fluctuations in debt follow two major cycles.

00:07:14.770 --> 00:07:20.830
One is between 5 and 8 years old and the other is between 75 and 100 years old, approximately.

00:07:20.830 --> 00:07:25.160
Although most people feel the ups and downs, they don't usually see them as cycles

00:07:25.160 --> 00:07:29.490
because they see them too closely: day by day, week by week.

00:07:29.490 --> 00:07:32.950
In this chapter we will take a step back to look at these three great forces

00:07:32.950 --> 00:07:37.000
and how they interact to create our experiences.

00:07:37.400 --> 00:07:42.340
We saw that the fluctuations around the line don't arise from how much innovation or hard work there is,

00:07:42.340 --> 00:07:46.240
but mostly from how much credit there is.

00:07:46.240 --> 00:07:50.070
Let's imagine for a moment an economy without credit.

00:07:50.070 --> 00:07:53.810
In this economy, I can only increase my expenses

00:07:53.810 --> 00:07:55.480
if I increase my income,

00:07:55.480 --> 00:07:59.080
which requires me to be more productive and work more.

00:07:59.080 --> 00:08:03.020
Higher productivity is the only path to growth.

00:08:03.020 --> 00:08:05.250
Since my expenses are someone else's income,

00:08:05.250 --> 00:08:10.430
the economy grows every time I, or anyone else, is more productive.

00:08:10.430 --> 00:08:13.530
If we track the transactions and display them,

00:08:13.530 --> 00:08:16.910
we see a progression like the productivity growth line.

00:08:16.910 --> 00:08:20.780
But because we take out loans, we have cycles.

00:08:20.780 --> 00:08:23.900
This does not arise from any law or regulation,

00:08:23.900 --> 00:08:27.660
but from human nature and how credit works.

00:08:27.660 --> 00:08:32.270
Think of taking out loans as stretching expenses forward.

00:08:32.270 --> 00:08:37.479
To buy something we can't afford, we have to spend more than we earn.

00:08:37.479 --> 00:08:41.460
To do this, we must borrow from our own future.

00:08:41.460 --> 00:08:44.610
This creates a time in the future

00:08:44.610 --> 00:08:48.690
in which we must spend less than we earn in order to pay it back.

00:08:48.690 --> 00:08:51.950
Very quickly it resembles a cycle.

00:08:51.950 --> 00:08:55.400
Essentially, every loan we take out creates a cycle.

00:08:55.400 --> 00:08:59.950
This is as true for an individual as it is for the economy.   That's why

00:08:59.950 --> 00:09:01.850
understanding credit is so important,

00:09:01.850 --> 00:09:04.590
because it sets in motion a series

00:09:04.590 --> 00:09:08.790
of future, mechanical, and predictable events.

00:09:08.790 --> 00:09:11.570
This is what differentiates credit from money.

00:09:13.470 --> 00:09:14.580
Money is what we use to settle transactions.

00:09:14.580 --> 00:09:18.120
When we buy a beer with cash,

00:09:18.120 --> 00:09:20.800
the transaction is settled immediately.

00:09:20.800 --> 00:09:22.630
On the other hand, if we buy it on credit,

00:09:22.630 --> 00:09:24.730
it's like opening an account at the bar.

00:09:24.730 --> 00:09:27.530
We promise to pay in the future.

00:09:27.530 --> 00:09:32.070
And together with the bartender we created an asset and a liability.

00:09:32.070 --> 00:09:35.250
We just created credit.  Out of nowhere.

00:09:35.250 --> 00:09:38.900
Only later, when the bar bill is paid, do

00:09:38.900 --> 00:09:41.250
the asset and liability disappear,

00:09:41.250 --> 00:09:42.550
the debt vanishes,

00:09:42.650 --> 00:09:44.090
and the transaction is settled.

00:09:45.090 --> 00:09:50.250
In reality, what most people call "money" is actually "credit".

00:09:50.250 --> 00:09:55.510
Total credit in the United States is about 50 trillion dollars

00:09:55.510 --> 00:09:59.680
and total circulating money is only about 3 trillion.

00:09:59.680 --> 00:10:02.040
Remember: in an economy without credit,

00:10:02.040 --> 00:10:05.200
the only way to spend more is to produce more.

00:10:05.400 --> 00:10:07.280
But in a credit-driven economy, we

00:10:07.280 --> 00:10:10.780
can also spend more by taking out loans.

00:10:10.780 --> 00:10:14.120
As a result, in an economy with credit, spending is higher,

00:10:14.120 --> 00:10:18.090
allowing income to rise faster than productivity in the short term,

00:10:18.090 --> 00:10:19.890
but not in the long term.

00:10:20.090 --> 00:10:21.360
Now then: don't misunderstand me;

00:10:21.560 --> 00:10:25.660
Credit is not necessarily a bad thing that only causes cycles.

00:10:25.660 --> 00:10:30.900
It is bad when it finances excessive consumption that cannot be repaid.

00:10:30.900 --> 00:10:34.490
However, it is good when it allocates resources effectively

00:10:34.490 --> 00:10:37.690
and generates income that allows us to repay the debt.

00:10:37.690 --> 00:10:40.980
For example, if we take out a loan to buy a large TV,

00:10:40.980 --> 00:10:43.879
the device does not generate income for us to repay the loan.

00:10:44.079 --> 00:10:47.880
But if we take out a loan to buy a tractor,

00:10:48.380 --> 00:10:51.560
so we can harvest larger crops and earn more money,

00:10:51.560 --> 00:10:53.090
we can repay the loan

00:10:53.090 --> 00:10:56.490
and improve our standard of living.

00:10:56.490 --> 00:10:57.600
In an economy with credit,

00:10:57.600 --> 00:10:59.600
we can track transactions

00:10:59.600 --> 00:11:01.300
and see how credit generates growth.

00:11:01.400 --> 00:11:03.680
Let's look at an example:

00:11:04.080 --> 00:11:08.190
Suppose we earn one hundred thousand dollars annually and have no debt.

00:11:08.390 --> 00:11:11.540
So, they'll lend us ten thousand dollars,

00:11:11.540 --> 00:11:13.040
let's say on a credit card,

00:11:13.040 --> 00:11:15.250
so we can spend 110,000 dollars

00:11:15.250 --> 00:11:18.250
even though we only actually earn 100,000.

00:11:18.750 --> 00:11:21.460
Since my expenses are someone else's income,

00:11:21.460 --> 00:11:24.460
someone is earning $110,000.

00:11:25.060 --> 00:11:27.670
A person earning $110,000

00:11:27.770 --> 00:11:31.429
with no debt can borrow $11,000

00:11:31.429 --> 00:11:34.070
and thus spend $121,000

00:11:34.070 --> 00:11:38.170
even though they only earned $110,000.

00:11:38.170 --> 00:11:40.640
Their expenses are someone else's income,

00:11:40.640 --> 00:11:43.430
and by following the transactions

00:11:43.430 --> 00:11:45.430
we can begin to see how this process

00:11:45.430 --> 00:11:47.430
follows a self-reinforcing pattern.

00:11:47.830 --> 00:11:51.600
But let's remember that taking out loans creates cycles,

00:11:51.600 --> 00:11:55.809
and if the cycle goes up, at some point it must go down.

00:11:56.009 --> 00:11:59.710
This brings us to the short-term debt cycle.

00:12:00.810 --> 00:12:04.380
When economic activity increases, we see an expansion

00:12:04.380 --> 00:12:06.840
- the first phase of the short-term debt cycle.

00:12:06.840 --> 00:12:10.720
Spending continues to grow and prices are starting to rise.

00:12:10.720 --> 00:12:15.230
This happens because increased spending is fueled by credit

00:12:15.230 --> 00:12:17.450
- which can be created instantly, out of thin air.

00:12:17.450 --> 00:12:22.680
When spending and income increase faster than the production of goods,

00:12:22.680 --> 00:12:23.880
prices rise.

00:12:23.880 --> 00:12:28.000
The rise in prices is called "inflation".

00:12:28.000 --> 00:12:32.500
The central bank does not want too much inflation

00:12:32.500 --> 00:12:35.010
because it causes problems.

00:12:35.010 --> 00:12:38.250
Seeing that prices are rising, the Bank raises interest rates.

00:12:38.250 --> 00:12:42.959
With higher interest rates, fewer people can afford to take out loans.

00:12:42.959 --> 00:12:45.600
And the cost of existing debts rises.

00:12:45.600 --> 00:12:50.100
For example, our monthly credit card payments are going up.

00:12:50.100 --> 00:12:54.459
As people borrow less and have to pay more for their debts,

00:12:54.459 --> 00:12:58.720
they have less money available to spend and spending slows down

00:12:58.720 --> 00:13:02.779
...and as one person's expenses are another person's income,

00:13:02.779 --> 00:13:06.589
incomes go down...and so on.

00:13:06.589 --> 00:13:10.800
When people spend less, prices go down.

00:13:10.800 --> 00:13:12.689
That's called "deflation".

00:13:12.689 --> 00:13:17.460
Economic activity is declining and we are experiencing a recession.

00:13:17.460 --> 00:13:20.060
If the recession becomes too severe

00:13:20.060 --> 00:13:22.440
and inflation is no longer a problem,

00:13:22.440 --> 00:13:27.060
the central bank will lower interest rates to get things back on track.

00:13:27.060 --> 00:13:29.650
With low interest rates,

00:13:29.650 --> 00:13:30.650
debt repayments decrease,

00:13:30.650 --> 00:13:33.250
more is borrowed and more is spent,

00:13:33.250 --> 00:13:35.450
and we see another expansion.

00:13:35.450 --> 00:13:39.450
As we can see, the economy works like a machine.

00:13:40.250 --> 00:13:44.160
In the short-term debt cycle, spending is restricted only by the willingness of

00:13:44.160 --> 00:13:47.140
lenders and borrowers to give and receive credit.

00:13:47.140 --> 00:13:52.510
When credit is readily available, there is economic expansion.

00:13:52.510 --> 00:13:56.090
When something is not easily obtained, there is a recession.

00:13:56.090 --> 00:14:00.450
And note: this cycle is primarily controlled by the central bank.

00:14:00.450 --> 00:14:05.120
The short-term debt cycle typically lasts 5 to 8 years

00:14:05.120 --> 00:14:08.410
and repeats itself over and over again for decades.

00:14:08.410 --> 00:14:10.410
But you will notice that the bottom and

00:14:10.410 --> 00:14:12.400
top of each cycle end

00:14:12.400 --> 00:14:16.670
with greater growth than the previous cycle and with more debt.

00:14:16.670 --> 00:14:18.040
Because?

00:14:18.040 --> 00:14:20.740
Because people push it

00:14:20.740 --> 00:14:25.530
— they are more inclined to take out loans and spend than to pay off debts.

00:14:25.530 --> 00:14:27.480
It's human nature.   That's why

00:14:27.480 --> 00:14:29.380
,

00:14:29.380 --> 00:14:30.680
in the long run,

00:14:30.680 --> 00:14:33.470
debts grow faster than incomes,

00:14:33.470 --> 00:14:36.670
creating the long-term debt cycle.

00:14:38.070 --> 00:14:39.670
Although people are more indebted,

00:14:40.170 --> 00:14:44.030
lenders are giving credit more easily.

00:14:44.030 --> 00:14:45.030
Because?

00:14:45.030 --> 00:14:48.670
Because everyone thinks things are going very well.

00:14:48.670 --> 00:14:51.970
People only think about recent events.

00:14:51.970 --> 00:14:55.650
And what happened recently?

00:14:55.650 --> 00:14:57.650
Revenues increased!

00:14:57.650 --> 00:14:59.250
Asset values ​​are rising!

00:14:59.250 --> 00:15:01.550
The stock market is roaring!   It

00:15:01.550 --> 00:15:02.480
's a boom!   It's a

00:15:02.480 --> 00:15:06.690
good idea to buy goods, services, and financial assets

00:15:06.690 --> 00:15:08.390
with borrowed money!

00:15:08.390 --> 00:15:11.770
When people do a lot of that, we call it "a bubble".

00:15:11.770 --> 00:15:14.640
So even though debts have been growing,

00:15:14.640 --> 00:15:18.110
income has grown almost as fast as to offset them.   I

00:15:18.110 --> 00:15:21.970
will call the relationship between debt and income "debt burden".

00:15:21.970 --> 00:15:25.210
As long as income continues to rise,

00:15:25.210 --> 00:15:27.040
the debt burden remains manageable.

00:15:27.040 --> 00:15:30.230
Simultaneously, asset values ​​are rising rapidly.

00:15:30.230 --> 00:15:33.230
People take out huge loans

00:15:34.030 --> 00:15:35.060
to buy investment assets,

00:15:35.060 --> 00:15:37.660
driving their price even higher.

00:15:37.660 --> 00:15:40.260
People feel rich.

00:15:40.260 --> 00:15:43.260
So even with a lot of accumulated debt,

00:15:43.260 --> 00:15:49.490
rising income and assets help borrowers remain creditworthy for a long time.

00:15:49.490 --> 00:15:52.530
Obviously, it's something that can't go on forever.

00:15:52.530 --> 00:15:54.250
And it doesn't happen.

00:15:54.250 --> 00:16:00.520
Over the decades, the debt burden slowly increases and creates growing repayments.

00:16:00.520 --> 00:16:05.400
When these refunds start to grow faster than income,

00:16:05.400 --> 00:16:08.240
they force people to cut back on their spending.

00:16:08.240 --> 00:16:12.200
And since one person's expenses are another person's income,

00:16:12.200 --> 00:16:14.500
incomes begin to fall...

00:16:14.500 --> 00:16:19.020
...which makes people less creditworthy and reduces the loans they take out.

00:16:19.020 --> 00:16:22.020
Debt repayments continue to rise,

00:16:22.020 --> 00:16:24.440
causing expenses to fall even further...

00:16:24.440 --> 00:16:27.140
and the cycle to reverse.

00:16:27.140 --> 00:16:30.960
This is the peak of long-term debt.

00:16:30.960 --> 00:16:34.150
The debt burdens have become too great.

00:16:34.150 --> 00:16:38.830
In the United States, Europe, and much of the world, this

00:16:38.830 --> 00:16:40.760
happened in 2008.

00:16:40.760 --> 00:16:45.430
It happened for the same reason as in Japan in 1989

00:16:45.430 --> 00:16:48.750
and in the United States in 1929.

00:16:48.750 --> 00:16:51.980
The economy is now beginning to deleverage.

00:16:51.980 --> 00:16:56.500
In this phase, people reduce spending,

00:16:56.500 --> 00:16:59.540
income falls, credit disappears,

00:16:59.540 --> 00:17:02.700
assets decline, banks suffer,

00:17:02.700 --> 00:17:06.340
stock markets collapse, social tensions increase,

00:17:06.340 --> 00:17:09.709
and everything begins to feed back in the opposite direction.

00:17:09.709 --> 00:17:13.850
As incomes fall and debt payments rise,

00:17:13.850 --> 00:17:17.990
borrowers become restricted.  Having run out of credit,

00:17:17.990 --> 00:17:22.360
their credit lines are exhausted and they can no longer borrow enough money to

00:17:22.360 --> 00:17:23.600
pay off their debts.

00:17:23.600 --> 00:17:28.440
Desperate, borrowers are forced to sell assets.

00:17:28.440 --> 00:17:31.550
The sales frenzy floods the market.

00:17:31.550 --> 00:17:36.370
This is where the stock market collapses,

00:17:36.370 --> 00:17:39.740
the real estate market plummets, and things get complicated for the banks.

00:17:39.740 --> 00:17:44.940
When asset prices fall, the value of borrowers' collateral also falls.

00:17:44.940 --> 00:17:48.280
This makes borrowers even less deserving of credit.

00:17:48.280 --> 00:17:50.200
People feel poor.

00:17:51.300 --> 00:17:55.010
Credit disappears quickly.  Less spending &gt;

00:17:55.010 --> 00:17:55.890
less income &gt;

00:17:55.890 --> 00:17:57.890
less wealth &gt;

00:17:57.890 --> 00:17:58.590
less credit &gt;

00:17:58.590 --> 00:18:00.800
fewer loans taken out and so on.

00:18:00.800 --> 00:18:03.000
It's a vicious cycle.   It

00:18:03.000 --> 00:18:06.530
resembles a recession, but the difference here

00:18:06.530 --> 00:18:10.160
is that interest rates cannot be lowered to save the situation.

00:18:10.160 --> 00:18:14.590
In a recession, lower interest rates encourage borrowing.

00:18:14.590 --> 00:18:18.570
But in a deleveraging process, lowering rates is useless because

00:18:18.570 --> 00:18:20.530
interest rates are already

00:18:20.530 --> 00:18:25.310
low and will soon reach 0%, so the stimulus ends.

00:18:25.310 --> 00:18:29.210
Interest rates in the United States reached 0% during the deleveraging

00:18:29.210 --> 00:18:30.840
of the 1930s

00:18:30.840 --> 00:18:33.440
and again in 2008.

00:18:33.440 --> 00:18:35.840
The difference between a recession

00:18:35.840 --> 00:18:40.270
and deleveraging is that in the latter, the debt burden on borrowers

00:18:40.270 --> 00:18:41.930
grew too large

00:18:41.930 --> 00:18:45.050
and cannot be relieved by lowering interest rates.

00:18:45.050 --> 00:18:50.240
Lenders understand that such excessive debts cannot be fully repaid.

00:18:50.240 --> 00:18:55.310
Borrowers lost their ability to pay and their guarantees lost value.   They

00:18:55.310 --> 00:18:59.370
feel paralyzed by debt and don't want any more.

00:18:59.370 --> 00:19:03.490
Lenders stop lending.  Borrowers stop borrowing.

00:19:03.490 --> 00:19:07.020
Let's think of the economy as unworthy of credit,

00:19:07.020 --> 00:19:09.000
like a person.   So what should be

00:19:09.000 --> 00:19:12.600
done about deleveraging?

00:19:12.600 --> 00:19:17.540
The problem is that debt burdens are very high and need to be reduced.

00:19:17.540 --> 00:19:20.790
There are four ways this can happen.

00:19:20.790 --> 00:19:24.489
One: People, businesses, and governments are cutting back on spending.

00:19:24.489 --> 00:19:28.409
Two: debts are reduced through moratoriums and restructurings.

00:19:28.409 --> 00:19:34.139
Three: wealth is redistributed from "those who have" to "those who do not have".

00:19:34.139 --> 00:19:37.980
Four: the central bank prints new money.

00:19:37.980 --> 00:19:42.999
These four forms occurred in every deleveraging of modern history.

00:19:45.699 --> 00:19:47.779
It is common to cut expenses first.

00:19:47.879 --> 00:19:52.129
As we have seen, individuals, companies, banks, and even governments are tightening their belts

00:19:52.129 --> 00:19:54.869
and cutting expenses in order to pay off their debts.

00:19:54.869 --> 00:19:58.559
This is often called "austerity".

00:19:58.559 --> 00:20:01.830
When borrowers stop taking on new debt

00:20:01.830 --> 00:20:06.769
and start paying off old debt, the debt burden can be expected to decrease.

00:20:06.769 --> 00:20:10.720
But the opposite happens!  Since spending is cut

00:20:10.720 --> 00:20:14.610
and one person's spending is another person's income, this causes

00:20:14.610 --> 00:20:19.029
income to fall.  Faster than debts are paid off,

00:20:19.029 --> 00:20:23.210
and so the debt burden actually worsens.  As we saw,

00:20:23.210 --> 00:20:26.330
this spending cut is deflationary and painful.

00:20:26.330 --> 00:20:29.389
Companies are forced to cut costs...

00:20:29.389 --> 00:20:32.779
which means fewer jobs and higher unemployment.

00:20:32.779 --> 00:20:37.139
This leads to the next stage: debts must be reduced!

00:20:37.139 --> 00:20:41.149
Many cannot repay the loans they took out

00:20:41.149 --> 00:20:44.299
– and the borrower's debts are the lender's asset.

00:20:44.299 --> 00:20:48.749
When borrowers fail to repay the bank, people fear that the bank will not

00:20:48.749 --> 00:20:50.309
be able to pay them what they are owed

00:20:50.309 --> 00:20:54.909
and rush to withdraw their money from the bank.  Banks are restricted and

00:20:54.909 --> 00:20:55.590
people,

00:20:55.590 --> 00:21:00.080
businesses and banks stop paying their debts.  This severe

00:21:00.080 --> 00:21:03.570
economic contraction is a depression.   A

00:21:03.570 --> 00:21:08.649
large part of depression involves discovering that what we assumed

00:21:08.649 --> 00:21:11.089
was our wealth, is not there.

00:21:11.089 --> 00:21:13.490
Let's go back to the bar.

00:21:13.690 --> 00:21:17.619
When we bought a beer on credit,

00:21:17.619 --> 00:21:23.570
we promised to pay the bar owner.  Our promise became an asset for the bartender.

00:21:23.570 --> 00:21:28.129
If we don't keep our promise - if we don't pay him and go into moratorium

00:21:28.129 --> 00:21:29.309
on our bar account -

00:21:29.309 --> 00:21:32.649
then this "asset" that he had is, in fact, worthless.

00:21:32.649 --> 00:21:35.779
In essence, it has disappeared.

00:21:35.779 --> 00:21:39.710
Many lenders do not want to see their assets disappear and agree to

00:21:39.710 --> 00:21:40.759
restructure the debt.

00:21:40.759 --> 00:21:44.320
This means that lenders will receive a

00:21:44.320 --> 00:21:48.049
smaller refund, or will be paid over a longer period

00:21:48.049 --> 00:21:52.330
or with a lower interest rate than initially agreed.  In any case,

00:21:52.330 --> 00:21:56.889
a contract is breached in order to reduce the debt.  Lenders thus prefer

00:21:56.889 --> 00:21:59.419
a little of something, rather than total nothingness.

00:21:59.419 --> 00:22:03.450
Although debt disappears, its restructuring causes

00:22:03.450 --> 00:22:06.559
the values ​​of income and assets to disappear faster,

00:22:06.559 --> 00:22:09.679
thus worsening the debt burden.

00:22:09.679 --> 00:22:12.950
Like cutting spending, reducing debt

00:22:12.950 --> 00:22:16.009
is also painful and deflationary.

00:22:16.009 --> 00:22:21.940
All of this affects the central government because lower revenues and less employment

00:22:21.940 --> 00:22:27.049
mean that the government collects less tax revenue.

00:22:27.049 --> 00:22:30.210
At the same time, it needs to increase its spending due to rising unemployment.

00:22:30.210 --> 00:22:33.960
Many of the unemployed have inadequate savings.

00:22:33.960 --> 00:22:36.419
and they need financial support from the government.

00:22:36.419 --> 00:22:39.999
In addition, governments create incentive plans

00:22:39.999 --> 00:22:43.879
and increase their spending to compensate for the economic downturn.

00:22:43.879 --> 00:22:47.509
Government budget deficits grow explosively during

00:22:47.509 --> 00:22:51.059
deleveraging because they spend more than they collect in taxes.

00:22:51.059 --> 00:22:55.720
That's what's happening when we hear about the news deficit.

00:22:55.720 --> 00:23:01.320
To finance deficits, governments must raise taxes

00:23:01.320 --> 00:23:06.059
or borrow money.  But with falling revenues and so many unemployed, where will

00:23:06.059 --> 00:23:09.789
the money come from?  From the rich.

00:23:09.789 --> 00:23:14.580
Since governments need more money and wealth is highly concentrated

00:23:14.580 --> 00:23:17.109
in the hands of a small percentage of people, it

00:23:17.109 --> 00:23:20.379
is natural for governments to tax the wealthy more,

00:23:20.379 --> 00:23:24.450
which facilitates a redistribution of wealth in the economy -

00:23:24.450 --> 00:23:29.279
from "those who have" to "those who do not."  Those who lack and suffer begin to

00:23:29.279 --> 00:23:30.970
feel resentment towards the rich "who have".

00:23:30.970 --> 00:23:36.309
Those "who have," suffering from a weak economy, falling asset prices,

00:23:36.309 --> 00:23:39.940
and rising taxes, begin to resent "those who do not have."

00:23:39.940 --> 00:23:43.890
If depression continues, social order may be disrupted.

00:23:43.890 --> 00:23:47.280
Not only will internal tensions rise within countries,

00:23:47.280 --> 00:23:52.200
but also between countries, especially between debtors and creditors.

00:23:52.200 --> 00:23:56.040
This situation can lead to political change

00:23:56.040 --> 00:23:58.840
that can sometimes be extreme.

00:23:58.840 --> 00:24:02.630
In the 1930s, this led to Hitler's rise to power,

00:24:02.630 --> 00:24:08.080
war in Europe, and the depression in the United States.  Pressure is mounting to do something

00:24:08.080 --> 00:24:10.070
to end depression.

00:24:10.070 --> 00:24:14.950
Let's remember: what most people considered money was actually credit.

00:24:14.950 --> 00:24:18.400
Thus, when credit disappears, people don't have enough money.

00:24:18.400 --> 00:24:23.190
People are desperate for money.  Do you remember who can print it?

00:24:23.190 --> 00:24:27.370
The central bank.

00:24:27.370 --> 00:24:30.309
Having already lowered its interest rates to almost zero,

00:24:30.309 --> 00:24:34.090
it is forced to print money.  Unlike spending cuts,

00:24:34.090 --> 00:24:37.299
debt reduction, and wealth redistribution,

00:24:37.299 --> 00:24:42.190
printing money is inflationary and stimulating.  Inevitably, the central bank

00:24:42.190 --> 00:24:43.210
prints new money

00:24:43.210 --> 00:24:47.150
— out of thin air — which it uses to buy financial assets

00:24:47.150 --> 00:24:52.260
and government bonds.  It happened in the United States during the Great Depression

00:24:52.260 --> 00:24:56.010
and again in 2008, when its central bank,

00:24:56.010 --> 00:24:59.940
the Federal Reserve, printed more than two trillion dollars.

00:24:59.940 --> 00:25:04.179
Other central banks around the world that could, also printed a lot of money.

00:25:04.179 --> 00:25:07.440
Buying financial assets with this money

00:25:07.440 --> 00:25:11.679
helps raise asset prices, making people more creditworthy.

00:25:11.679 --> 00:25:15.820
But this only helps those who have financial assets.

00:25:15.820 --> 00:25:21.840
The central bank can print money, but it can only buy financial assets.

00:25:21.840 --> 00:25:24.900
The central government, for its part,

00:25:24.900 --> 00:25:29.580
can buy goods and services and give money to people

00:25:29.580 --> 00:25:34.520
but cannot print money.  So, to stimulate the economy, the two

00:25:34.520 --> 00:25:35.650
must cooperate.

00:25:35.650 --> 00:25:40.470
By buying government bonds, the central bank essentially lends money to the

00:25:40.470 --> 00:25:41.190
government,

00:25:41.190 --> 00:25:44.909
allowing it to maintain a deficit and increase spending

00:25:44.909 --> 00:25:48.650
on goods and services through its incentive

00:25:48.650 --> 00:25:53.080
and unemployment insurance programs.  This increases people's income

00:25:53.080 --> 00:25:56.240
as well as the government's debt.  However,

00:25:56.240 --> 00:25:59.540
it will reduce the overall debt burden on the economy.

00:25:59.540 --> 00:26:05.429
This is a moment of great risk.  Policymakers must balance the four ways in which

00:26:05.429 --> 00:26:06.590
the debt burden is reduced.

00:26:06.590 --> 00:26:13.050
Deflationary policies must balance inflationary policies

00:26:13.050 --> 00:26:14.710
to maintain stability.

00:26:14.710 --> 00:26:18.130
If they are balanced correctly, a

00:26:18.130 --> 00:26:21.479
beautiful deleveraging can occur.   The thing is,

00:26:21.479 --> 00:26:25.359
deleveraging can be ugly or beautiful.

00:26:25.359 --> 00:26:28.839
How can it be beautiful?

00:26:28.839 --> 00:26:33.429
Although deleveraging is a difficult situation,

00:26:33.429 --> 00:26:37.549
optimally managing a difficult situation is a beautiful thing.

00:26:37.549 --> 00:26:42.210
Much more beautiful than the unbalanced, debt-fueled excesses of

00:26:42.210 --> 00:26:45.940
the leverage phase.  In a beautiful deleveraging,

00:26:45.940 --> 00:26:51.089
debts decrease relative to incomes, real economic growth is positive,

00:26:51.089 --> 00:26:56.889
and inflation is not a problem.  It is achieved with the correct balance.

00:26:56.889 --> 00:27:00.159
The right balance requires a certain mix

00:27:00.159 --> 00:27:04.229
of spending cuts, debt reduction, wealth transfer,

00:27:04.229 --> 00:27:09.149
and money printing to maintain economic and social stability.

00:27:09.149 --> 00:27:13.639
People are asking if printing money will cause inflation.

00:27:13.639 --> 00:27:18.879
No, if it compensates for the credit that falls.  Remember: spending is what matters.

00:27:18.879 --> 00:27:24.249
A dollar spent with cash affects prices just as much as a dollar

00:27:24.249 --> 00:27:25.969
spent with credit.

00:27:25.969 --> 00:27:31.299
By printing money, the central bank can compensate for the disappearance of credit

00:27:31.299 --> 00:27:33.080
with an increase in the money supply.

00:27:33.080 --> 00:27:38.509
To turn things around, the central bank needs not only to encourage

00:27:38.509 --> 00:27:39.519
income growth

00:27:39.519 --> 00:27:43.849
but also to ensure that the rate of income growth exceeds the interest rate

00:27:43.849 --> 00:27:45.099
on accumulated debt.

00:27:45.099 --> 00:27:48.379
What do I mean by that?  Essentially,

00:27:48.379 --> 00:27:52.309
revenue must grow faster than debt.  For example:

00:27:52.309 --> 00:27:56.080
suppose a deleveraging country has a

00:27:56.080 --> 00:27:58.580
debt-to-income ratio of 100%.

00:27:58.580 --> 00:28:03.659
This means that their total debt is equal to the income

00:28:03.659 --> 00:28:05.799
of the entire country in one year.

00:28:05.799 --> 00:28:09.009
Let's now think about the interest rate on that debt, let's say

00:28:09.009 --> 00:28:11.159
2%.

00:28:11.159 --> 00:28:15.029
If debt grows at 2% at that interest rate and

00:28:15.029 --> 00:28:15.690
income

00:28:15.690 --> 00:28:20.230
grows at only 1%, the debt burden will never be reduced.

00:28:20.230 --> 00:28:24.809
Enough money needs to be printed so that the rate of income growth exceeds

00:28:24.809 --> 00:28:25.950
the interest rate.

00:28:25.950 --> 00:28:30.720
However, it is easy to abuse money printing because it is so easy, and

00:28:30.720 --> 00:28:33.049
because people prefer it to alternatives.

00:28:33.049 --> 00:28:36.200
The key is to avoid printing too much money

00:28:36.200 --> 00:28:41.110
and causing unacceptably high inflation, as Germany did

00:28:41.110 --> 00:28:43.129
during its deleveraging in the 1920s.

00:28:43.129 --> 00:28:48.269
If policymakers strike the right balance, deleveraging is not so dramatic.

00:28:48.269 --> 00:28:51.340
Growth is slow, but debt burdens are decreasing.

00:28:51.340 --> 00:28:54.470
This is a beautiful deleveraging process.

00:28:54.470 --> 00:28:59.830
With incomes rising, borrowers begin to look more creditworthy.

00:28:59.830 --> 00:29:02.999
And when this happens,

00:29:02.999 --> 00:29:08.509
lenders start lending again.  Debt burdens are finally starting to decrease.

00:29:08.509 --> 00:29:13.499
By being able to take out loans, people can spend more.  The economy is now beginning to

00:29:13.499 --> 00:29:14.399
grow again, moving

00:29:14.399 --> 00:29:18.009
towards the reflation phase of the long-term debt cycle.

00:29:18.009 --> 00:29:22.490
Although deleveraging can be terrible if handled poorly,

00:29:22.490 --> 00:29:26.279
when handled properly, it will solve the problem.

00:29:26.279 --> 00:29:29.580
It takes about ten years or more for

00:29:29.580 --> 00:29:33.669
the burdens to decrease and for economic activity to return to normal

00:29:33.669 --> 00:29:37.899
- hence the expression "lost decade".

00:29:37.899 --> 00:29:42.720
Of course, economics is a bit more complex than this template

00:29:42.720 --> 00:29:43.639
suggests.

00:29:43.639 --> 00:29:48.850
But by superimposing the short-term debt cycle onto the long-term debt cycle,

00:29:48.850 --> 00:29:52.629
and both above the productivity growth line,

00:29:52.629 --> 00:29:55.919
we get a reasonable model for seeing where we have been,

00:29:55.919 --> 00:29:58.919
where we are, and where we are likely to go.

00:29:58.919 --> 00:30:03.019
In summary, there are three practical rules I would like you to take away

00:30:03.019 --> 00:30:03.720
from this talk:

00:30:03.720 --> 00:30:08.220
The first: Don't let debt grow faster than income,

00:30:08.220 --> 00:30:11.429
because the burden of debt will eventually crush you.

00:30:11.429 --> 00:30:16.059
The second: That revenues do not grow faster than productivity,

00:30:16.059 --> 00:30:19.159
because that will make us uncompetitive.

00:30:19.159 --> 00:30:24.000
And the third: do everything possible to increase productivity,

00:30:24.000 --> 00:30:29.029
because in the long run that is what matters most.   These are

00:30:29.549 --> 00:30:34.080
simple tips for everyone, including policymakers.

00:30:34.080 --> 00:30:38.470
Surprisingly, most people — and policymakers — don't pay enough attention

00:30:38.470 --> 00:30:39.509
to this.

00:30:39.509 --> 00:30:44.059
This template has worked for me and I hope it will work for you.

00:30:44.059 --> 00:30:46.380
Thank you so much!

